## Building a REST API with ASP.NET Core – A Complete “From Scratch” Guide

> **TL;DR**  
> 1. Create a new ASP.NET Core project (`dotnet new webapi`).  
> 2. Design your domain (models, DTOs).  
> 3. Add a persistence layer (EF Core + DbContext).  
> 4. Implement a Repository / Service layer.  
> 5. Create Controllers (or Minimal APIs).  
> 6. Wire up DI, validation, logging, Swagger, CORS, authentication.  
> 7. Test (Postman + integration tests).  
> 8. Dockerize, CI/CD, deploy.

Below is a **step‑by‑step walkthrough** that covers both the *classic controller* approach and the *minimal API* approach that is now the recommended style in ASP.NET Core 6/7. Pick the one that suits your project’s needs.

---

## 1. Set Up Your Project

```bash
# Create a new API project with EF Core (SQL Server) template
dotnet new webapi -n MyRestApi
cd MyRestApi
```

The default template already includes:

- `Program.cs` (the host + middleware pipeline)
- `WeatherForecastController.cs` (an example controller)
- `appsettings.json`
- `appsettings.Development.json`
- `WeatherForecast.cs` (a sample model)
- `WeatherForecastController` (uses EF Core in memory by default)

> **Tip**: If you’re using a database other than SQL Server, add the appropriate provider (e.g., `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite`).

---

## 2. Domain Design

### 2.1 Domain Models

```csharp
// Domain/Models/Book.cs
namespace MyRestApi.Domain.Models
{
    public class Book
    {
        public Guid Id { get; set; }      // Primary key
        public string Title { get; set; } = null!;
        public string Author { get; set; } = null!;
        public DateTime Published { get; set; }
        public decimal Price { get; set; }
    }
}
```

### 2.2 DTOs (Data Transfer Objects)

We usually expose DTOs instead of domain entities to avoid leaking internal structure and to control serialization.

```csharp
// Application/DTOs/BookDto.cs
namespace MyRestApi.Application.DTOs
{
    public record BookDto(Guid Id, string Title, string Author, DateTime Published, decimal Price);
}
```

### 2.3 AutoMapper Profiles (optional but handy)

```csharp
// Application/Mapping/BookMappingProfile.cs
using AutoMapper;
using MyRestApi.Domain.Models;
using MyRestApi.Application.DTOs;

public class BookMappingProfile : Profile
{
    public BookMappingProfile()
    {
        CreateMap<Book, BookDto>();
        CreateMap<BookDto, Book>();
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Persistence Layer

### 3.1 DbContext

```csharp
// Persistence/BookStoreContext.cs
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using MyRestApi.Domain.Models;

namespace MyRestApi.Persistence
{
    public class BookStoreContext : DbContext
    {
        public BookStoreContext(DbContextOptions<BookStoreContext> options)
            : base(options) { }

        public DbSet<Book> Books => Set<Book>();

        protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
        {
            modelBuilder.Entity<Book>(entity =>
            {
                entity.HasKey(b => b.Id);
                entity.Property(b => b.Title).IsRequired().HasMaxLength(200);
                entity.Property(b => b.Author).IsRequired().HasMaxLength(200);
                entity.Property(b => b.Published).IsRequired();
                entity.Property(b => b.Price).IsRequired().HasColumnType("decimal(18,2)");
            });

            // Seed data (optional)
            modelBuilder.Entity<Book>().HasData(
                new Book { Id = Guid.NewGuid(), Title = "Clean Code", Author = "Robert C. Martin", Published = new DateTime(2008, 8, 1), Price = 42.99m },
                new Book { Id = Guid.NewGuid(), Title = "Domain-Driven Design", Author = "Eric Evans", Published = new DateTime(2003, 8, 30), Price = 49.99m }
            );
        }
    }
}
```

### 3.2 Add EF Core to the Project

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design
dotnet add package AutoMapper.Extensions.Microsoft.DependencyInjection
```

Add the connection string to `appsettings.json`:

```json
{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "Server=(localdb)\\mssqllocaldb;Database=BookStoreDb;Trusted_Connection=True;MultipleActiveResultSets=true"
  }
}
```

---

## 4. Repository / Service Layer

You can choose to expose a thin **repository** (data access) or a more involved **service** (business logic). For a simple CRUD API a repository is enough.

```csharp
// Application/Interfaces/IBookRepository.cs
using MyRestApi.Domain.Models;

namespace MyRestApi.Application.Interfaces
{
    public interface IBookRepository
    {
        Task<IEnumerable<Book>> GetAllAsync(CancellationToken ct = default);
        Task<Book?> GetByIdAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct = default);
        Task<Book> AddAsync(Book book, CancellationToken ct = default);
        Task<Book?> UpdateAsync(Book book, CancellationToken ct = default);
        Task<bool> DeleteAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct = default);
    }
}
```

```csharp
// Persistence/Repositories/BookRepository.cs
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using MyRestApi.Application.Interfaces;
using MyRestApi.Domain.Models;

namespace MyRestApi.Persistence.Repositories
{
    public class BookRepository : IBookRepository
    {
        private readonly BookStoreContext _context;

        public BookRepository(BookStoreContext context)
        {
            _context = context;
        }

        public async Task<IEnumerable<Book>> GetAllAsync(CancellationToken ct = default)
            => await _context.Books.AsNoTracking().ToListAsync(ct);

        public async Task<Book?> GetByIdAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct = default)
            => await _context.Books.FindAsync(new object[] { id }, ct);

        public async Task<Book> AddAsync(Book book, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            _context.Books.Add(book);
            await _context.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
            return book;
        }

        public async Task<Book?> UpdateAsync(Book book, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            var existing = await _context.Books.FindAsync(new object[] { book.Id }, ct);
            if (existing is null) return null;
            _context.Entry(existing).CurrentValues.SetValues(book);
            await _context.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
            return existing;
        }

        public async Task<bool> DeleteAsync(Guid id, CancellationToken ct = default)
        {
            var entity = await _context.Books.FindAsync(new object[] { id }, ct);
            if (entity is null) return false;
            _context.Books.Remove(entity);
            await _context.SaveChangesAsync(ct);
            return true;
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 5. API Layer

### 5.1 Classic Controllers

```csharp
// Controllers/BooksController.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using MyRestApi.Application.DTOs;
using MyRestApi.Application.Interfaces;
using AutoMapper;

namespace MyRestApi.Controllers
{
    [ApiController]
    [Route("api/[controller]")]
    public class BooksController : ControllerBase
    {
        private readonly IBookRepository _repo;
        private readonly IMapper _mapper;

        public BooksController(IBookRepository repo, IMapper mapper)
        {
            _repo = repo;
            _mapper = mapper;
        }

        // GET: /api/books
        [HttpGet]
        public async Task<ActionResult<IEnumerable<BookDto>>> GetAll()
        {
            var books = await _repo.GetAllAsync();
            var dtos = _mapper.Map<IEnumerable<BookDto>>(books);
            return Ok(dtos);
        }

        // GET: /api/books/{id}
        [HttpGet("{id:guid}")]
        public async Task<ActionResult<BookDto>> Get(Guid id)
        {
            var book = await _repo.GetByIdAsync(id);
            if (book == null) return NotFound();
            return Ok(_mapper.Map<BookDto>(book));
        }

        // POST: /api/books
        [HttpPost]
        public async Task<ActionResult<BookDto>> Create([FromBody] BookDto dto)
        {
            var book = _mapper.Map<MyRestApi.Domain.Models.Book>(dto);
            book.Id = Guid.NewGuid();   // generate server-side ID
            await _repo.AddAsync(book);
            return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = book.Id }, _mapper.Map<BookDto>(book));
        }

        // PUT: /api/books/{id}
        [HttpPut("{id:guid}")]
        public async Task<ActionResult<BookDto>> Update(Guid id, [FromBody] BookDto dto)
        {
            if (id != dto.Id) return BadRequest("ID mismatch");

            var book = _mapper.Map<MyRestApi.Domain.Models.Book>(dto);
            var updated = await _repo.UpdateAsync(book);
            if (updated == null) return NotFound();

            return Ok(_mapper.Map<BookDto>(updated));
        }

        // DELETE: /api/books/{id}
        [HttpDelete("{id:guid}")]
        public async Task<IActionResult> Delete(Guid id)
        {
            var deleted = await _repo.DeleteAsync(id);
            if (!deleted) return NotFound();
            return NoContent();
        }
    }
}
```

**What this controller demonstrates:**

- **Routing** with attribute routing.
- **HTTP verbs** (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE).
- **Status codes** (`200 OK`, `201 Created`, `204 No Content`, `404 Not Found`, `400 Bad Request`).
- **Model validation** – you can add `[FromBody]` DTOs with data‑annotation attributes for automatic validation.

### 5.2 Minimal APIs (Recommended in .NET 7+)

If you prefer less ceremony, you can use *minimal APIs* – everything lives in `Program.cs`.

```csharp
// Program.cs (minimal API version)
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using MyRestApi.Persistence;
using MyRestApi.Application.Interfaces;
using MyRestApi.Persistence.Repositories;
using AutoMapper;
using MyRestApi.Application.DTOs;
using MyRestApi.Domain.Models;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Add services to the container.
builder.Services.AddDbContext<BookStoreContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")));

builder.Services.AddScoped<IBookRepository, BookRepository>();
builder.Services.AddAutoMapper(typeof(Program)); // auto-detect profiles
builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();
builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseSwagger();
app.UseSwaggerUI();

app.MapGet("/api/books", async (IBookRepository repo, IMapper mapper) =>
{
    var books = await repo.GetAllAsync();
    return Results.Ok(mapper.Map<IEnumerable<BookDto>>(books));
});

app.MapGet("/api/books/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, IBookRepository repo, IMapper mapper) =>
{
    var book = await repo.GetByIdAsync(id);
    return book is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(mapper.Map<BookDto>(book));
});

app.MapPost("/api/books", async (BookDto dto, IBookRepository repo, IMapper mapper) =>
{
    var book = mapper.Map<Book>(dto);
    book.Id = Guid.NewGuid();
    await repo.AddAsync(book);
    return Results.Created($"/api/books/{book.Id}", mapper.Map<BookDto>(book));
});

app.MapPut("/api/books/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, BookDto dto, IBookRepository repo, IMapper mapper) =>
{
    if (id != dto.Id) return Results.BadRequest("ID mismatch");
    var updated = await repo.UpdateAsync(mapper.Map<Book>(dto));
    return updated is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(mapper.Map<BookDto>(updated));
});

app.MapDelete("/api/books/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, IBookRepository repo) =>
{
    var deleted = await repo.DeleteAsync(id);
    return deleted ? Results.NoContent() : Results.NotFound();
});

app.Run();
```

**Benefits of Minimal APIs**

- Zero‑config controllers.
- Less boilerplate.
- Straightforward to add *endpoints* that act like small lambda functions.
- Still supports DI, validation, middleware, Swagger, etc.

---

## 6. Wiring It All Together

### 6.1 Dependency Injection

In the *classic* controller approach, you register everything in `Program.cs` (or `Startup.cs` if you prefer the older style).

```csharp
builder.Services.AddDbContext<BookStoreContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection")));

builder.Services.AddScoped<IBookRepository, BookRepository>();
builder.Services.AddAutoMapper(typeof(Program));   // loads profiles
builder.Services.AddControllers();
```

### 6.2 Migration & Database Initialization

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate
dotnet ef database update
```

If you want to seed data programmatically on startup, use `IHostedService` or add a `DbInitializer` in `Program.cs`.

---

## 7. Cross‑Cutting Concerns

| Concern | Implementation | Why |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Validation** | Data annotations on DTOs, `IValidator<T>` with FluentValidation, `IActionFilter` | Prevents bad data from reaching the business layer. |
| **Authentication / Authorization** | JWT bearer, ASP.NET Identity, Azure AD | Secure endpoints. |
| **CORS** | `builder.Services.AddCors(...)` | Allows browser clients from different origins. |
| **Logging** | `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging` + Serilog / NLog | Diagnostics. |
| **Exception Handling** | `app.UseExceptionHandler` or a custom `IExceptionFilter` | Centralized error response. |
| **Rate Limiting** | `AspNetCoreRateLimit` | Protects APIs from abuse. |
| **Compression** | `app.UseResponseCompression()` | Improves bandwidth usage. |
| **Health Checks** | `app.MapHealthChecks("/health")` | For Kubernetes / load balancer liveness probes. |

---

## 8. Swagger / OpenAPI

The template already adds Swagger. After the service registration above, you’ll see a nice interactive UI at `/swagger`. It auto‑documents your endpoints, including response types and possible status codes.

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen(c =>
{
    c.SwaggerDoc("v1", new OpenApiInfo
    {
        Title = "BookStore API",
        Version = "v1",
        Description = "A sample REST API built with ASP.NET Core"
    });

    // Add JWT support if needed
    c.AddSecurityDefinition("Bearer", new OpenApiSecurityScheme
    {
        In = ParameterLocation.Header,
        Description = "JWT Authorization header using the Bearer scheme. Example: \"Bearer {token}\"",
        Name = "Authorization",
        Type = SecuritySchemeType.ApiKey
    });

    c.AddSecurityRequirement(new OpenApiSecurityRequirement
    {
        {
            new OpenApiSecurityScheme
            {
                Reference = new OpenApiReference { Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme, Id = "Bearer" }
            },
            Array.Empty<string>()
        }
    });
});
```

